      {
        "id": "thm-qa-27-cks-linear-condition-guarantee",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "CKS nearly-linear condition-number guarantee",
        "deps": [
          "thm-qa-relative-probability-guarantee",
          "lem-qa-27-low-target-amplification",
          "lem-qa-27-capped-lower-bound-amplification",
          "thm-qa-24-qsvt-query-count",
          "thm-qa-27-pseudoinverse-polynomial-degree",
          "alg-qa-27-cks-variable-time-linear-solver",
          "def-qa-27-cks-sparse-oracle-model",
          "lem-qa-27-cks-stopping-time-instantiation",
          "thm-qa-25-qubitized-simulation-resource-bound",
          "thm-qa-25-sparse-hamiltonian-block-encoding-correctness",
          "def-qc-dirac-notation-conversion"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_level": 26,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let $A$ be an $N\\times N$ Hermitian matrix with at most $d$ nonzero entries in each row, $\\|A\\|_{\\rm op}\\le1$, supplied with the full sparse access model of [[def-qa-27-cks-sparse-oracle-model]]. Its finite-precision values must decode to a Hermitian $\\widetilde A$ with certified $\\|A-\\widetilde A\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\epsilon_{\\rm val}$, and the exact QA25 encoding and simulator-call certificates must satisfy the budgets in [[alg-qa-27-cks-variable-time-linear-solver]]. Let $|b\\rangle$ be a normalized state supported on eigenvalues with $|\\lambda|\\ge1/\\kappa$ for declared $\\kappa\\ge1$, prepared by a supplied clean-work unitary $B$. For targets $0<\\epsilon<1$ and $0<\\rho<1/3$, execute the constructor at supplied rationals $\\epsilon/2<e\\le\\epsilon$ and $\\rho/2<\\beta\\le\\rho$, with access and construction certificates for these requests. The uncapped `efficient-qsvt` constructor, or a finite-cap construction that completes its certificates, outputs with probability at least $1-\\rho$ a conditional pure system state admitting a unit representative within vector-norm error $\\epsilon$ of $A^+|b\\rangle/\\|A^+|b\\rangle\\|$, for every fixed $0<\\rho<1/3$. Here the output is conditioned on its classical execution transcript: for any unit representative $\\psi$ of its ray, there exists $\\omega\\in\\mathbb C$, $|\\omega|=1$, such that $\\|\\omega\\psi-A^+|b\\rangle/\\|A^+|b\\rangle\\|\\|\\le\\epsilon$. This terminal-state phase convention does not change any coherent stage, block-encoding, or controlled-operation phase specified by the access contract.\n\nFor the prescribed precision schedule and least admissible OP1A degree bounds just specified, the query bound is $O\\!\\left(d\\kappa\\,\\operatorname{poly}\\!\\left(\\log\\frac{d\\kappa}{\\epsilon},\\log\\frac1\\rho\\right)\\right)$ calls in each declared row-location, reverse-location, value, and input-preparation query coordinate; inverse and coherent forms retain their separate coordinates. A construction-cap exhaustion returns its declared timeout and is not included in this success guarantee. If the support of $|b\\rangle$ has the smaller condition number $\\kappa'<\\kappa$, $\\kappa$ may be replaced by $\\kappa'$. The output is a quantum state; no classical coordinate list is promised.\n\nThe separate gate-efficient conclusion holds only under this explicit gate specialization: every required forward, inverse and controlled form of $B$, row-location, reverse-location, value access, and the $\\widetilde A/d$ block encoding has a uniform circuit using at most $g_0=\\operatorname{polylog}(N,d,\\kappa,1/\\epsilon,1/\\rho)$ gates per call; state preparation, selected/input reflections, reversible clock predicates, dilution rotations, and the final zero-work instrument and controlled reflections also use at most $g_0$ gates per invocation; and each QA25 simulation at time $t$ and clean-input error $\\xi$ has a uniform circuit using $O((d|t|+\\log(1/\\xi))g_0)$ gates, with the same bound for every required controlled or inverse form. The QFTs, reversible phase comparisons and majority circuits, and efficient-grid rounding circuits used by the probability estimator must also have uniform polylogarithmic gate counts at every allowed scale. A uniform phase compiler must implement each QSVT phase within its allocated error using at most $g_0$ gates. Under these conditions the total quantum gate count is $O(d\\kappa\\,\\operatorname{polylog}(N,d,\\kappa,1/\\epsilon,1/\\rho))$. Query bounds remain separate from these gate bounds. Classical polynomial/phase generation, exact algebraic comparison, median/schedule arithmetic, arithmetic bit implementation and compiler construction costs remain separate coordinates; no polylogarithmic total bit-time bound is inferred."
      }
